`main()` walks the samples three at a time. x and y are `binary::f64_from_bits` of the two patterns. It prints one line: the function name (sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, log2, log10, atan2, hypot, pow), a space, x's pattern as 16 lowercase hex digits with leading zeros, a space, and for the two-argument functions (codes 8 to 10) y's pattern the same way followed by a space, then the result: "nan" when it is NaN, else its pattern as 16 lowercase hex digits.
